#{[17592186119503 :question/worked-solution "The determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ -4 & 1 & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ 1 & 10 & \\sqrt{3} & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ -3 & 0 & 6 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & =(-2)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-2)=4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119504 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $1$st column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $1$st column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & {\\color{lightgray}{4}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 13 & 19 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 13 & 19 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 13 & 19 & -3\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 13 & 19 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot (-3)=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = 2 \\cdot (-6) = -12.\n$$"] [17592186119506 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}{:}$\n\n$$\nM =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}2 & 3 & - 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ - 4 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & - 4 & 2 & 5\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M{:}$\n\n$$\nV = | \\det(M) |\n$$\n\nNotice that the $2$nd row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{2}} & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{-4}} & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & -4 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $2$nd row of the $3 \\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & {\\color{lightgray}{-2}} & 1 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 1 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ -4 & {\\color{lightgray}{2}} & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3(15+4) \\\\  & =-57.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV = | \\det(M) | = |-57| = 57.\n$$"] [17592186120154 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186120155 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186120155 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=5\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)=-30.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-30|=30.\n$$"] [17592186120429 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $3$rd column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 0 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & {\\color{lightgray}{11}} & 1 & {\\color{lightgray}{19}} \\\\ 4 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot (-3)=-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(B)=-9.\n$$"] [17592186265875 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $3$rd column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 0 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{-4}} & {\\color{lightgray}{1}} & 2 & {\\color{lightgray}{8}} \\\\ 9 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\cdot 5\\cdot 6\\cdot (-1)=-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(B)=-60.\n$$"] [17592186265879 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $4$th column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $4$th column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(E) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{\\frac{7}{4}}} & {\\color{lightgray}{\\frac{3}{5}}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 6 & -5 & -\\frac{1}{4} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{2}{5} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{8}{3} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-1)^{1+4}\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -5 & -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{8}{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & -5 & -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{8}{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & -5 & -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{8}{3}\\end{vmatrix} & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (6\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3})=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(E)=-2.\n$$"] [17592186265881 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $3$rd row contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(C) & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & {\\color{lightgray}{8}} & 5 & 6 \\\\ 7 & {\\color{lightgray}{-4}} & 1 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 4 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ 3 & {\\color{lightgray}{-2}} & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $3$rd row of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{5}} & 5 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{7}} & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot (3\\cdot (-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\  & =-12\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-12(10-6) \\\\  & =(-12)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186265883 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $4$th column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $4$th column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(S) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 & 2 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ 5 & -7 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $3$rd column of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{-3}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-1}} & 2 \\\\ 5 & -7 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ 0 & 2 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6(10-0) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186275444 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186275444 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 7 & 13 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 7 & 13 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 2=24.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|24|=24.\n$$"] [17592186275445 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186342239 :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186342239 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}5 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 2 & -1 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & k+6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & k+6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+4\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k+4.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k+4\\,⇒\\,k=-4.\n$$"] [17592186342240 :question/problem "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\mathrm{Π}:\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}$, $t,s\\in \\mathbb{R}$, where  $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-1,2,3⟩$, $\\mathbf{c}=⟨3,-7,-9⟩$, and  $\\mathbf{u}=⟨2,-3,-6⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{u}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}.$"] [17592186342240 :question/worked-solution "By definition, $\\mathbf{u}\\in \\mathrm{Π}$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{u} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+3t &=2 \\\\ 2s-7t &=-3 \\\\ 3s-9t &=-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -9 & -6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -9 & -6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+3t &=2 \\\\ -t &=1 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-t=1\\,⟹\\,t=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-s+3t & =2 \\\\ -s+3(-1) & =2 \\\\ -s & =5 \\\\ s & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=-5,\\,t=-1,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{u}$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=-5\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{c}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=(-5)\\cdot (-1)=5.$"] [17592186342241 :question/problem "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\mathrm{Π}:\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}$, $t,s\\in \\mathbb{R}$, where  $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-1,4,3⟩$, $\\mathbf{c}=⟨1,-6,-7⟩$, and  $\\mathbf{u}=⟨1,-10,-15⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{u}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}.$"] [17592186342241 :question/worked-solution "By definition, $\\mathbf{u}\\in \\mathrm{Π}$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{u} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+t &=1 \\\\ 4s-6t &=-10 \\\\ 3s-7t &=-15\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -6 & -10 \\\\ 3 & -7 & -15\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 3 & -7 & -15\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -12\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+t &=1 \\\\ -2t &=-6 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-2t=-6\\,⟹\\,t=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-s+t & =1 \\\\ -s+3 & =1 \\\\ -s & =-2 \\\\ s & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=2,\\,t=3,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{u}$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=2\\mathbf{b}+3\\mathbf{c}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=2\\cdot 3=6.$"] [17592186342243 :question/problem "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\mathrm{Π}:\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}$, $t,s\\in \\mathbb{R}$, where  $\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,7,-6⟩$, $\\mathbf{c}=⟨-2,-10,12⟩$, and  $\\mathbf{u}=⟨2,11,-12⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{u}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}.$"] [17592186342243 :question/worked-solution "By definition, $\\mathbf{u}\\in \\mathrm{Π}$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{u} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s-2t &=2 \\\\ 7s-10t &=11 \\\\ -6s+12t &=-12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 7 & -10 & 11 \\\\ -6 & 12 & -12\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 12 & -12\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s-2t &=2 \\\\ 4t &=-3 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n4t=-3\\,⟹\\,t=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s-2t & =2 \\\\ s-2\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right) & =2 \\\\ s+\\frac{3}{2} & =2 \\\\ s & =2-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ s & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=\\frac{1}{2},\\,t=-\\frac{3}{4},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{u}$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}-\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf{c}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle s\\cdot t=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)=-\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$"] [17592186344944 :question/problem "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\mathrm{Π}:\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c},$ $t,s\\in \\mathbb{R}$, where  $\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,3,6⟩$, $\\mathbf{c}=⟨1,-2,4⟩$, and  $\\mathbf{u}=⟨3,19,-10⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{u}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}.$"] [17592186344944 :question/worked-solution "By definition, $\\mathbf{u}\\in \\mathrm{Π}$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{u} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 19 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s+t &=3 \\\\ 3s-2t &=19 \\\\ 6s+4t &=-10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 19 \\\\ 6 & 4 & -10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 6 & 4 & -10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -28\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -28\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -32\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=-32,$ which has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of $s$ and $t$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc=u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude 