\mathbf{c}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=3\\cdot 2=6.$"] [17592186399461 :question/problem "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\mathrm{Π}:\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}$, $t,s\\in \\mathbb{R}$, where  $\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,-4,2⟩$, $\\mathbf{c}=⟨3,-10,2⟩$, and  $\\mathbf{u}=⟨-2,5,0⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{u}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}.$"] [17592186399461 :question/worked-solution "By definition, $\\mathbf{u}\\in \\mathrm{Π}$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{u} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s+3t &=-2 \\\\ -4s-10t &=5 \\\\ 2s+2t &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -10 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=-2,$ which has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of $s$ and $t$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc=u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\mathbf{u}$ does *not* lie on the plane $Π.$"] [17592186403766 :question/problem "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\mathrm{Π}:\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}$, $t,s\\in \\mathbb{R}$, where  $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-1,4,-5⟩$, $\\mathbf{c}=⟨3,-9,15⟩$, and  $\\mathbf{u}=⟨2,-2,11⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{u}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}.$"] [17592186403766 :question/worked-solution "By definition, $\\mathbf{u}\\in \\mathrm{Π}$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{u} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+3t &=2 \\\\ 4s-9t &=-2 \\\\ -5s+15t &=11\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -9 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 11\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 11\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=1,$ which has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of $s$ and $t$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc=u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\mathbf{u}$ does *not* lie on the plane $Π.$"]}
